<p>One of the most common questions after a storm claim in Texas is: "Why is my check so much smaller than the estimate?" The answer is almost always the difference between <strong>replacement cost vs. actual cash value</strong>, plus the deductible. Understanding how depreciation works, when it comes back, and when it does not can help you plan repairs and spot problems in a settlement.</p>

<p>This guide is written for property owners in San Marcos, Hays County and across Central Texas. It explains the concepts in plain English and includes a clearly labeled hypothetical example. Every policy is different, so treat this as background and read your own policy for the rules that apply to you.</p>

<h2>Replacement cost vs. actual cash value: the basic definitions</h2>

<p><strong>Replacement cost value (RCV)</strong> is what it would cost today to repair or replace damaged property with new materials of like kind and quality, at current local prices for labor and materials.</p>

<p><strong>Actual cash value (ACV)</strong> is replacement cost minus depreciation. Depreciation is the reduction in value for age, wear and condition. A 15-year-old roof is not worth what a brand-new one is, and ACV reflects that.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Replacement cost (RCV) coverage</th><th>Actual cash value (ACV) coverage</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>What it aims to pay</td><td>The cost to repair or replace with new, like-kind materials</td><td>Replacement cost minus depreciation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical first payment</td><td>ACV minus deductible</td><td>ACV minus deductible</td></tr>
<tr><td>Depreciation</td><td>Often recoverable after repairs are completed</td><td>Generally not recoverable</td></tr>
<tr><td>Repair requirement</td><td>Usually must complete repairs to collect the full amount</td><td>Usually no repair requirement to receive ACV</td></tr>
<tr><td>Premium</td><td>Usually higher</td><td>Usually lower</td></tr>
<tr><td>Where to find it</td><td>Declarations page and loss settlement section</td><td>Declarations page, loss settlement section and endorsements</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>How depreciation is calculated</h2>

<p>Insurers commonly estimate depreciation by comparing an item’s age with its expected useful life, then adjusting for condition. For a roof, the adjuster may consider the age of the roof covering, the type of material, and any visible wear. Estimating software then applies a percentage to the affected line items.</p>

<p>A few points worth knowing:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Age matters, so prove it.</strong> If the insurer assumes your roof is older than it is, depreciation will be too high. Permits, invoices, home inspection reports or closing documents can establish the real age.</li>
<li><strong>Condition matters.</strong> A well-maintained roof may deserve less depreciation than an estimate assumes.</li>
<li><strong>Not every line item depreciates the same way.</strong> Look at which items carry depreciation and at what percentage. Ask the adjuster to explain anything that seems inconsistent.</li>
<li><strong>Depreciation can be questioned.</strong> It is a judgment, and reasonable people can disagree about it.</li>
</ul>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/stock/homeowners-reviewing-claim-costs.webp" alt="Couple reviewing costs with a professional at a table with a calculator" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>The estimate summary page shows RCV, depreciation, ACV and the deductible. Read it line by line.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Recoverable vs. non-recoverable depreciation</h2>

<p>On a replacement-cost policy, the insurer typically pays the ACV first. The depreciation it held back is called <strong>recoverable depreciation</strong> (sometimes "holdback"). After you complete the repairs, you submit proof, and the insurer releases the remaining amount, generally up to the lesser of the replacement cost in the estimate or what you actually spent.</p>

<p><strong>Non-recoverable depreciation</strong> is depreciation you cannot get back. It applies when the property is covered on an ACV basis, when a roof payment schedule applies, or when a policy condition for recovering depreciation is not met.</p>

<h3>Deadlines for recoverable depreciation</h3>
<p>Many replacement-cost policies require you to complete repairs and notify the insurer within a stated time to collect the holdback. The deadline is in the loss settlement section or a related condition. Some insurers will extend it if you ask in writing before it expires and explain the reason, such as a contractor backlog after a widespread hailstorm. Do not assume an extension; ask for it in writing and keep the response.</p>

<h3>What insurers usually want to release depreciation</h3>
<ul>
<li>A final invoice from the contractor showing the completed work and cost</li>
<li>Photos of the completed repairs</li>
<li>A signed contract or scope of work, if requested</li>
<li>Permit and inspection documents, if a permit was required</li>
</ul>
<p>If your contractor found additional damage or code items during the work, submit those as a supplement with photos before or along with the final invoice. Our page on <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplements</a> explains how that works.</p>

