<h2>A worked example (hypothetical)</h2>

<div class="callout callout-gold"><span class="callout-title">Hypothetical example, for illustration only</span><p>The numbers below are invented to show how the math works. They are not typical costs, depreciation rates or settlement amounts, and they do not describe any real claim. Your policy, deductible and estimate will be different.</p></div>

<p>Imagine a single-family home with a dwelling limit (Coverage A) of $300,000 and a wind/hail deductible of 2% of Coverage A, which is $6,000. Hail damages the composition shingle roof. The roof covering is 12 years old, and the insurer’s estimate prices the full roof replacement at $20,000 RCV and applies 48% depreciation.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Replacement-cost policy</th><th>ACV roof coverage</th><th>Roof payment schedule (assume 60% of RCV for this roof age)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Replacement cost (RCV)</td><td>$20,000</td><td>$20,000</td><td>$20,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Depreciation / schedule reduction</td><td>−$9,600 (held back)</td><td>−$9,600</td><td>−$8,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Amount before deductible</td><td>$10,400 (ACV)</td><td>$10,400 (ACV)</td><td>$12,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deductible</td><td>−$6,000</td><td>−$6,000</td><td>−$6,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>First payment</td><td>$4,400</td><td>$4,400</td><td>$6,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Released after repairs</td><td>Up to $9,600</td><td>$0</td><td>$0</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Total possible from insurer</strong></td><td><strong>Up to $14,000</strong></td><td><strong>$4,400</strong></td><td><strong>$6,000</strong></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Three lessons come out of this example:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>The first check can look alarmingly small</strong> on a replacement-cost policy. Here it is $4,400 against a $20,000 roof. The rest arrives only after the work is done.</li>
<li><strong>The coverage type changes the outcome more than almost anything else.</strong> Same damage, same estimate, very different totals.</li>
<li><strong>Every input matters.</strong> If the estimate missed line items, the RCV is too low, and every column shrinks with it. If the roof’s age was overstated, depreciation is too high. That is why reviewing the estimate itself, not just the payment, is so important. See <a href="/how-to-review-underpaid-roof-insurance-estimate/">how to review an underpaid roof estimate</a>.</li>
</ol>

