<h2>Roof payment schedules and ACV roof endorsements in Texas</h2>

<p>Some Texas homeowners policies treat the roof differently from the rest of the house. Two common approaches:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>ACV roof coverage.</strong> The dwelling is covered at replacement cost, but the roof surface (and sometimes related items) is settled at actual cash value. Depreciation on the roof is not recoverable.</li>
<li><strong>Roof payment schedules.</strong> The policy pays a set percentage of the roof’s replacement cost based on the roof’s age and sometimes its material. The schedule replaces the usual depreciation calculation, and the reduction is generally not recoverable.</li>
</ul>

<p>These provisions are often found in endorsements, not on the first page of the policy. If you live in an area with frequent hail, as much of Hays County is, it is worth knowing before a storm whether your roof is covered at replacement cost. NOAA’s <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Storm Events Database</a> lists 180 hail reports in Hays County since 1967, most of them in April and May. See our <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a> page for more on local hail claims.</p>

<h2>Other things that affect what you are paid</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Deductibles.</strong> Many Texas policies carry a separate wind/hail deductible, often a percentage of the dwelling limit. Check your declarations page.</li>
<li><strong>Ordinance or law coverage.</strong> Building code upgrades are often paid under a separate, limited coverage, sometimes only after repairs are complete. Our article on <a href="/building-codes-insurance-repairs-san-marcos/">building codes and insurance repairs in San Marcos</a> explains how this works.</li>
<li><strong>Mortgage company endorsement.</strong> If you have a mortgage, checks may be payable to you and your lender, and the lender may control how funds are released.</li>
<li><strong>Policy limits.</strong> The total cannot exceed the applicable coverage limit.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How a San Marcos public adjuster can help</h2>

<p>Depreciation disputes usually come down to evidence: the real age and condition of the property, the completeness of the estimate, and the exact policy language. A <a href="/">San Marcos public adjuster</a> can read your loss settlement provisions and endorsements, check the depreciation applied to each line, identify missing items that lower the RCV, and help you track the paperwork needed to recover held-back depreciation before a deadline passes. Public adjusters represent the policyholder only and cannot give legal advice or guarantee a result.</p>

<p>If your settlement seems low, our <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a> and <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> pages explain what a review involves. A San Marcos public adjuster can also look at your estimate and payment letter through our <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>
